ing diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(- \\frac{3}{2})\\frac{y^{2}}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(- \\frac{5}{2},- \\frac{3}{2})$,$(\\frac{5}{2},- \\frac{5}{2})$,$(\\frac{7}{2},- \\frac{5}{2})$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(- \\frac{5}{2},- \\frac{3}{2})}=(- \\frac{3}{2})\\frac{(- \\frac{3}{2})^{2}}{(- \\frac{5}{2})}=(- \\frac{3}{2})\\frac{\\frac{9}{4}}{- \\frac{5}{2}}=\\frac{27}{20}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\frac{5}{2},- \\frac{5}{2})}=(- \\frac{3}{2})\\frac{(- \\frac{5}{2})^{2}}{(\\frac{5}{2})}=(- \\frac{3}{2})\\frac{\\frac{25}{4}}{\\frac{5}{2}}=- \\frac{15}{4}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\frac{7}{2},- \\frac{5}{2})}=(- \\frac{3}{2})\\frac{(- \\frac{5}{2})^{2}}{(\\frac{7}{2})}=(- \\frac{3}{2})\\frac{\\frac{25}{4}}{\\frac{7}{2}}=- \\frac{75}{28}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/2e/2e0a934b40d6ff2955cdbb9d4af2e5bf834ea59c13a212ccd128ffb04f2663e2.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526341 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06a901d5-ca4f-5737-bade-9a3f95e9fcd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/93/93ca31dd0832f9b09274e8357adfb1b27cefac07efc2ea1fdae1cadff7fa3b69.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526301} {:answer/id #uuid "2c0977f8-8622-51a6-88a6-3fc7164c99ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d8/d8014fb86ea2854bf8184fb5aa805fe0b6d3e1a8261622bbf6f5f619a8ce1ed2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526315} {:answer/id #uuid "4390a21d-ba70-57a9-93e0-edfc65e04c1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3d/3d41555957cd320f3631109842e318bb713699b0dcebc428f4fd644675c807a2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526324} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4f1112-d5b0-57c6-92ab-ecb8479ec106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9d/9d155a396810956274ddd882f0936851ff5cb5d8fce675323359f44b77e118d8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526339} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1aa56d-e965-583f-a3e3-be8c7e709980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/be/bed3530863181b65d9203e422502eabdffeb7edfb9a1d6ceff0f650024825ed5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd1aa56d-e965-583f-a3e3-be8c7e709980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/be/bed3530863181b65d9203e422502eabdffeb7edfb9a1d6ceff0f650024825ed5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526352} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a818083-44bf-57bd-b9d9-d68d87092f62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "997fd844-921f-59ba-966d-0ef1a0054bf4" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(- \\frac{3}{2})x^{2}-(-2)y^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(- \\frac{1}{2},- \\frac{7}{2})$,$(- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$,$(- \\frac{7}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(- \\frac{1}{2},- \\frac{7}{2})}=(- \\frac{3}{2})(- \\frac{1}{2})^{2}-(-2)(- \\frac{7}{2})^{2}=- \\frac{3}{8}-(- \\frac{49}{2})=\\frac{193}{8}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})}=(- \\frac{3}{2})(- \\frac{1}{2})^{2}-(-2)(\\frac{1}{2})^{2}=- \\frac{3}{8}-(- \\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{8}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(- \\frac{7}{2},\\frac{1}{2})}=(- \\frac{3}{2})(- \\frac{7}{2})^{2}-(-2)(\\frac{1}{2})^{2}=- \\frac{147}{8}-(- \\frac{1}{2})=- \\frac{143}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/be/bed3530863181b65d9203e422502eabdffeb7edfb9a1d6ceff0f650024825ed5.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac8b5d3-4035-5dd3-a5dd-ebf6500b19f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y}{y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526526} {:answer/id #uuid "1319139a-d5dc-5725-94ac-d3880fa8552c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526540} {:answer/id #uuid "39126990-5fa4-5f41-bc2f-d291064aff94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526610} {:answer/id #uuid "94939d5f-6f1b-5dd9-ac7a-3a24e4324e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526793} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe28192-dd92-5dbb-b0a6-33f806a9c962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fe28192-dd92-5dbb-b0a6-33f806a9c962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526811} :answer-field/id #uuid "021bcd34-e94e-5e91-97f6-6e6c378cff60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02209bdf-5c25-5d71-9f0c-90055349806b" :question/math-academy-id "q-159335" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{xy-y}{x}, & x\\ge 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & x\\ge 1,\\,y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{Y}(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y,$ for $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "The marginal CDF of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=F(\\infty ,y)={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{xy-y}{x}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{y(x-1)}{x}) \\\\  & =y{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =y{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1-0) \\\\  & =y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "540b5a5a-5141-58c1-9bbe-df63004cb07d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "540b5a5a-5141-58c1-9bbe-df63004cb07d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526674} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d582431-5271-591c-a589-e2a2aea28272" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "056f7c20-573d-55c9-bb54-7bee0a1479fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-185189" :question/problem "Expressed as an improper fraction or mixed number, ${(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To work out ${(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2},$ we multiply $\\frac{5}{3}$ by itself:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}=\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{3}=\\frac{25}{9}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31759e3d-c825-5447-bb62-62dc40ca4e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/50/50db86bd273e9e20ae705890103b067a1f664ed912fcb6efe9083db1060625c8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526599} {:answer/id #uuid "67b29a88-29cb-58f9-8069-b85a03c112ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9a/9a4300f10da578d54cda15c5ff69b433c462843671879194bfd79c3754f6cb5b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526707} {:answer/id #uuid "d63732c6-b997-5b18-9b93-6fe4ec0fd927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c1/c17f3162b8aae5a7ef077122de5e9eb0c8f2ce5763115940e602b695ad0da85e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526916} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c8fd91-21e4-51ad-b080-aaae59a800e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/20723072cad71ae40384b63840898d2239c916081995b8101be8a2f9980bb834.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526960} {:answer/id #uuid "f292085d-a5a2-50ac-9e18-2090bc014589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8f/8f8e5f15260cbff980164f132a380ad539785d77a92ce3be1d1f3f50e7101b5c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31759e3d-c825-5447-bb62-62dc40ca4e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/50/50db86bd273e9e20ae705890103b067a1f664ed912fcb6efe9083db1060625c8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526599} :answer-field/id #uuid "41456bc4-a176-526b-b41f-6dfca212c1d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "093fd520-21d8-5c0d-89e8-fac985077f71" :question/math-academy-id "q-123618" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams shows a unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of $\\frac{13\\pi }{9}$ in the standard position?" :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{13\\pi }{9})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })={260}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\frac{13\\pi }{9}$ is equivalent to ${260}^{∘}.$\n\nThen, recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- A positive angle has a rotation that is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/50/50db86bd273e9e20ae705890103b067a1f664ed912fcb6efe9083db1060625c8.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0153aeb0-58c4-5c73-8036-9df357871177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/73/73ab06d0194c397473319fcda2067761b8ac9b8d34333ffa1316552c4de8a8d0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526501} {:answer/id #uuid "7c125b62-5ac5-55f9-9f39-b1d75b192a8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b9/b912b5f205a6baf20a0e86d7bcb521651278db92af6480417ca1e8f2e0197382.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526747} {:answer/id #uuid "94c833a6-4aa0-58ca-9b4d-89b409187095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9d/9d811e234521fda8d08a58dd39c1fe6f3e8dce43a4e197a736c340fafd9649a5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526794} {:answer/id #uuid "dac4b509-3d83-5182-be7b-2caa46451772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3f/3f357f157e2274b4c15a336c8bf2f15a38d44c8e633cc74a4534931666e910cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526927} {:answer/id #uuid "ef42d8b8-af1b-506e-8041-6901cf5ee8e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6a/6ae77b6ce2d7f8c7541ee09c997dcbad86bc4aa40ff330a42c9c755271405796.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef42d8b8-af1b-506e-8041-6901cf5ee8e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6a/6ae77b6ce2d7f8c7541ee09c997dcbad86bc4aa40ff330a42c9c755271405796.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526976} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9033da7-e2fe-5546-be78-e76b67621ec0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0fb80f93-0987-58b5-ba12-42ac451dfbe6" :question/math-academy-id "q-317323" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+6?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/01/0183b9256896263aadfb0d18ae0a88aa11152c6e7e7d5693771f2e5bac20ac0a.png)\n\n \n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/31/31c5930bbeca76437d8e7a642bf52da4814bc5afda681b7974b383d3b8762c21.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $6$ units up to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+6.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,5)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=6.$\n\n![](images/c4/c4092a81a26d34360480e2ee46e99a17fa740b398038f36de427f3ed839bc68b.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da512ca-3f7f-55fa-ba92-0e0c6f6c21be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526563} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9f7694-d0a1-5a9c-8f7b-8ada526129f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{3v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526627} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e1f31b-7a73-5f60-aa37-ebb1960532b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{v}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526815} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ff2ce6-b380-5ab9-9fa2-41399e5c2bf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526881} {:answer/id #uuid "cabf98b2-4e8b-519a-86a9-7f344c20fcb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4ff2ce6-b380-5ab9-9fa2-41399e5c2bf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526881} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aa3cd04-7b64-5645-9bfe-bb149f8d1b73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "216ab53f-9c7f-5387-ad09-67452d907209" :question/math-academy-id "q-160572" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}xy \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=xy$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =x \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =\\frac{2x}{y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ \\frac{2x}{y} & -\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =y\\cdot (-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}})-x\\cdot \\frac{2x}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3v}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527154 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb37b488-9bf2-5a55-9b98-ff72718291b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb37b488-9bf2-5a55-9b98-ff72718291b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526893} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfa2a1c0-d3ce-5d21-872c-ede324a37869" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3cfc1cc8-75ac-58cb-baba-115d9b2db8af" :question/math-academy-id "q-185181" :question/problem "Expressed as an improper fraction or mixed number, ${(-\\frac{8}{5})}^{2}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To work out ${(-\\frac{8}{5})}^{2},$ we multiply $-\\frac{8}{5}$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-\\frac{8}{5})}^{2} &= (-\\frac{8}{5})\\cdot (-\\frac{8}{5}) \\\\ &= \\frac{8\\cdot 8}{5\\cdot 5} \\\\ &= \\frac{64}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "096fe8eb-0f73-5dcd-9283-5609e6cf6c6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{x{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526521} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7b246b-a91d-5397-9b3e-492593bcf3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526572} {:answer/id #uuid "9ffd77dc-caa5-5a7b-9077-2effd6a99afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526812} {:answer/id #uuid "c936279a-3486-5ccb-b9e0-fffe1ccea34f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526891} {:answer/id #uuid "f30d0020-1a54-5639-ab8d-73a8529f890a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ffd77dc-caa5-5a7b-9077-2effd6a99afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526812} :answer-field/id #uuid "20ab855f-f5a7-5070-a185-6c452a9551a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74fc0efc-8b80-569c-9c95-98f9405e5eca" :question/math-academy-id "q-159311" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)({y}^{2}-1)}{x{y}^{2}}, & x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 2$ and $y\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{(x-2)({y}^{2}-1)}{x{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-2}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-2}{x}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{2}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}, & x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527172 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "053308a9-7001-5846-9b1c-aebce9551076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526512} {:answer/id #uuid "45595bd3-a3a8-53e3-bf8d-e8a743e2acff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526636} {:answer/id #uuid "53f5cfc8-bae2-544a-87f1-7473d053b227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526673} {:answer/id #uuid "c04ad9e8-50e3-5c90-8826-dc328c55a80c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{4}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526869} {:answer/id #uuid "d0da0530-4038-57b2-ad9c-a85207241016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0da0530-4038-57b2-ad9c-a85207241016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526905} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc195312-1e76-599e-a6d3-b22fd38f25b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a3648d6-4396-5f3b-a4a5-7bdad067d750" :question/math-academy-id "q-138973" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y={sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\pi .$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot |2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527177 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1544ee01-f534-5f91-a989-82f6c4f8f30f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526544} {:answer/id #uuid "3ade37f7-6413-5d0c-aff6-f6ec6bccd435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526614} {:answer/id #uuid "3c40ec30-1fb8-58f2-8c9a-e6bd665ac483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526615} {:answer/id #uuid "a12ca628-2589-5dfb-9c58-44d876d2d743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526816} {:answer/id #uuid "a54b7301-a104-5d35-8c4d-e29e0811b8dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a54b7301-a104-5d35-8c4d-e29e0811b8dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526818} :answer-field/id #uuid "661c9719-3dbb-5353-ab1d-1d0798143c7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86ba6c5d-d640-5e57-88b7-9b3f13232b7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-159337" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y}{2\\pi }, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>2,\\,y\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{X}(x),$ the marginal CDF of $X,$ for $0\\le x\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=F(x,\\infty )={lim}_{y\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le x\\le 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & ={lim}_{y\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y}{2\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\pi }\\cdot {lim}_{y\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15aa0740-bb6f-50d4-a937-2834a1c7e53c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{x{e}^{-2y}}{2+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526547} {:answer/id #uuid "9664a9c8-6635-5edb-95e2-cc6d28ff1c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2+x}(1-{e}^{-2y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526797} {:answer/id #uuid "b69b91e9-4e20-5e6f-b2c3-445a82748c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{1+x}(1-{e}^{-2y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526854} {:answer/id #uuid "e34a28f2-4fe3-5a59-b680-84b2b53ea3c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{x}{2+x}-{e}^{-2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526948} {:answer/id #uuid "f54531dc-c152-57ba-b9ee-ccc647ffc034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2y}}{2+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9664a9c8-6635-5edb-95e2-cc6d28ff1c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2+x}(1-{e}^{-2y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526797} :answer-field/id #uuid "55ed3e7c-fab6-5103-98c8-80e32b0d59cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a27194f1-04ae-54c8-8fbb-2f24bc3ed222" :question/math-academy-id "q-159322" :question/problem "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2+x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-2y}, & y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{2+x})(1-{e}^{-2y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2+x}(1-{e}^{-2y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2+x}(1-{e}^{-2y}), & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "274904f4-58f7-56e2-9b6d-31c1ae57ce78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526587} {:answer/id #uuid "6431f8e0-22c1-5a92-b706-ab325a5dfa0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526703} {:answer/id #uuid "8776b217-a7c6-5847-98f1-2b8609aea8cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{90}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526772} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f1ac2b-edcc-5f11-8220-b6a757312e57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526822} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd5b701-3c55-5a13-9705-5e37e7b904b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffd5b701-3c55-5a13-9705-5e37e7b904b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527012} :answer-field/id #uuid "695816b9-028c-5cdf-b7e1-17fd412268d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b06c7090-b9ea-594c-b431-3fe9296b347e" :question/math-academy-id "q-99437" :question/problem "${(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "To work out \n${(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2},$\n we multiply \n$\\frac{1}{9}$\n by itself:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}=\\frac{1}{81}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527203 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dc087e2-0dbe-5157-ba6b-1c3b2fefb15b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526564} {:answer/id #uuid "71ff9bb7-a425-576c-95a3-1135fc029967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{35}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526724} {:answer/id #uuid "daa05632-592e-54c0-bc54-0039b3767877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526926} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a66eda-15a4-5835-97f8-9fb6a90c3584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526944} {:answer/id #uuid "f3668ebd-cf52-5aea-abde-8afb6d8922f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1a66eda-15a4-5835-97f8-9fb6a90c3584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526944} :answer-field/id #uuid "5397a46c-0c2d-53f8-ab44-aceeab82183b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba6955a5-f1b2-5bb6-bbb0-ee805b7e5b79" :question/math-academy-id "q-123640" :question/problem "![](images/ca/caacb6ef264b8f7862a893b4127704e46e66d33a212b53017e8bde4e32fa3004.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(\\sqrt{6},\\sqrt{2}),$ calculate the angle $\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/43/432dbcd0967ba40a5f477d30c8e9b0985c8151fb2f13e54ad346cff4ac20c1c5.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})={30}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527204 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3de31675-bc76-555b-9da6-89ac30830000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{215}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526620} {:answer/id #uuid "445205a4-1ab3-560d-a8b3-93a2255a869b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{240}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526634} {:answer/id #uuid "56d28aad-8782-5c87-aca9-e51ba344d3aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{225}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526680} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f77e82-19ee-5840-97b8-39b65196ac3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{220}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526823} {:answer/id #uuid "f4be7806-8136-5f6c-86c3-4b86a43642ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{230}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56d28aad-8782-5c87-aca9-e51ba344d3aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{225}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526680} :answer-field/id #uuid "12987a6c-aa03-52c4-b21a-1cc9b7be360a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ba6b8db9-c5f9-50b9-8e8a-d50f12658a32" :question/math-academy-id "q-34277" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1bc441dcf36de00672be1ac0ff5004e25ffb04d64788a86df3c849da0600794a.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(-\\frac{5}{4},-\\frac{5}{4}),$ calculate the angle $\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis. Let us call ${\\theta }^{′}$ the acute angle that is formed with the $x$-axis such that ${\\theta }^{′}+{180}^{∘}=\\theta $\n\n![](images/e3/e3abf66be20a5ab3ed5d0b8812b127d6336cb88f8369937d415c8918d020e45e.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }^{′}=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{4})}{(\\frac{5}{4})}=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n{\\theta }^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using $\\theta ={\\theta }^{′}+{180}^{∘},$ we get $\\theta ={45}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={225}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e1dd07c-e585-5339-8d34-c067ab9d0124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{u}{4}-1)(\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+u+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526565} {:answer/id #uuid "3386a987-4784-5171-a2e1-094255e1eb0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{u}{2}+1)(\\frac{{u}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{u}{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526601} {:answer/id #uuid "4982dc80-f4cf-5d3a-be46-4ba2743eda41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{u}{4}+1)(\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}-u+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526649} {:answer/id #uuid "74bc0359-d645-51ed-8e8a-283c5a90cded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{u}{2}-1)(\\frac{{u}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{u}{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526731} {:answer/id #uuid "7d464a43-e7cc-5052-8183-f2ac55c0f667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{u}{2}-1)(\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+u+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74bc0359-d645-51ed-8e8a-283c5a90cded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{u}{2}-1)(\\frac{{u}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{u}{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526731} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bf69bfe-f49c-586d-9712-8dbc31ce7c96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee3cc387-c8ba-5e21-b6bf-54f77512a2c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-112336" :question/problem "Factor $\\frac{{u}^{3}}{8}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The difference of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}-{b}^{3}=(a-b)({a}^{2}+ab+{b}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write our expression as a difference of cubes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{u}^{3}}{8}-1={(\\frac{u}{2})}^{3}-{1}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{u}^{3}}{8}-1 & ={(\\frac{u}{2})}^{3}-{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{u}{2}-1)({(\\frac{u}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{u}{2}\\cdot 1+{(1)}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{u}{2}-1)(\\frac{{u}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{u}{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "080f383a-cad7-506e-98f6-a6105a463e73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{w}-\\frac{1}{z})(\\frac{4}{{w}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{wz}+\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526519} {:answer/id #uuid "1f2f5fad-489c-5ef2-abc8-2ead566193c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{w}-\\frac{2}{z})(\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{wz}+\\frac{4}{{z}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526571} {:answer/id #uuid "76707eb8-870b-5c30-8e98-237eddb6e483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{w}+\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}})(\\frac{4}{{w}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{w{z}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{z}^{6}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526735} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd1b9a5-c274-5960-8128-6e38205fa037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{w}+\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}})(\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{w{z}^{3}}+\\frac{4}{{z}^{6}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526746} {:answer/id #uuid "8121d101-b037-5fe7-a7e1-a87bb7b493b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{w}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}})(\\frac{4}{{w}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{w{z}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{z}^{6}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8121d101-b037-5fe7-a7e1-a87bb7b493b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{w}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}})(\\frac{4}{{w}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{w{z}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{z}^{6}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526760} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8a54896-3654-52b8-b0f1-96c46cbcf220" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "efa83c70-ebda-58c7-9dc4-66acbcb8874b" :question/math-academy-id "q-112333" :question/problem "Factor $\\frac{8}{{w}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{9}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The difference of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}-{b}^{3}=(a-b)({a}^{2}+ab+{b}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write our expression as a difference of cubes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{{w}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{9}}={(\\frac{2}{w})}^{3}-{(\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}})}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{{w}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{9}} & ={(\\frac{2}{w})}^{3}-{(\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}})}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{w}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}})({(\\frac{2}{w})}^{2}+\\frac{2}{w}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{z}^{3}}+{(\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}})}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{w}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}})(\\frac{4}{{w}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{w{z}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{({z}^{3})^{2}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{w}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}})(\\frac{4}{{w}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{w{z}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{z}^{6}})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "575f89b2-b860-5bf6-94c2-b2386d5174b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{3{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526682} {:answer/id #uuid "ac47ec72-2d41-5b5a-b068-bbdef9147797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{3{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526834} {:answer/id #uuid "dd56e164-faac-55a7-ae60-d5adf3110db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16{x}^{3}}{9{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526935} {:answer/id #uuid "fa973044-c950-5daa-a2a0-d7518f5a10c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16x}{3{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527000} {:answer/id #uuid "fe148dd0-c4b8-52c9-9a1c-2693969bc24e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x}{9{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa973044-c950-5daa-a2a0-d7518f5a10c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16x}{3{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527000} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bb56fd2-9bf7-57d4-98c0-5fd980b76e4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f97149b0-2afa-55ad-bcc3-a7b6ba9e10a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-159331" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}({y}^{2}-1)}{3{y}^{2}}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y>2 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & x>1,\\,y>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{X}$ and ${F}_{Y}$ are the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Find the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is determined by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $1\\le y\\le 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{4{x}^{2}({y}^{2}-1)}{3{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3{y}^{3}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{16x}{3{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16x}{3{y}^{3}}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "236a2d72-bb0b-50be-bba3-e4d52248a15f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526581} {:answer/id #uuid "4b66b758-0826-5108-9ac0-ade8eefbc63c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526657} {:answer/id #uuid "71dd7185-fad4-53e7-b17c-bea3ed88b7f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526723} {:answer/id #uuid "b08f83bf-773c-562d-b48f-e6657ce17368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526838} {:answer/id #uuid "d77ff831-4178-5cb0-8b9c-9a40136d4cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b08f83bf-773c-562d-b48f-e6657ce17368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526838} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0c66bcf-f118-5a15-8c75-354e4b9c987f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcac8514-54e9-5652-a542-ae9e241a741a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159315" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)(y-1)}{xy}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{(x-1)(y-1)}{xy})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527922 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "041f8bb1-395a-5761-a87c-1b044587e237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527315} {:answer/id #uuid "5577dcb2-91aa-5255-8e1f-f21bfb8168c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527470} {:answer/id #uuid "62850344-0899-50d9-b54b-f07542ecb296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527493} {:answer/id #uuid "a64f5e23-38b3-551e-9f55-fba8ae74a486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\pi ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527651} {:answer/id #uuid "ab311e64-5391-5036-a765-998bb36b2623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab311e64-5391-5036-a765-998bb36b2623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527659} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9630a8b-9a3c-56a1-882a-b2e2265a7e4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1cf055f3-7724-5017-8bd4-b9d8370b23a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-122943" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{t}{\\pi }),\\,y=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\pi $ on the curve. Determine the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\pi $ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{\\pi })=4\\pi (0)=0, \\\\ y & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(0,-\\pi ).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{t}{\\pi })+4,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{t}{\\pi })+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\pi ,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{\\pi })+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{4\\cdot 0+4}=-\\,\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(-\\frac{1}{4})\\,}=4\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,-\\pi )$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-\\pi ) & =4(x-0) \\\\ y & =4x-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal intersects the $x$-axis when $y=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-\\pi  & =0 \\\\ 4x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ is the $x$-intercept."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cf8e160-2051-5377-beb9-7f358fa12cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527334} {:answer/id #uuid "ca613bca-62eb-519c-a990-d494e25f04b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527714} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd80d10-6b1c-5684-a3dc-37d716ebf7e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527727} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8fe0a7-5a62-53ed-9ad4-a69d4dc8c276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527795} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2d7ec6-a6e6-5a31-bdbf-78782edf7758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca613bca-62eb-519c-a990-d494e25f04b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527714} :answer-field/id #uuid "c14a0fdb-ff7e-57f0-b03c-3708c9d22c3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3104fe5f-1fab-5144-bd0b-8c85fa4d01fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-146225" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\frac{5}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=2]$ given that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|2}=\\frac{11}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $X,$ corresponding to the row totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\frac{5}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x:$\n\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=2] & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)]-{\\mu }_{Y|2}^{2} \\\\  & =[{2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+{4}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+{8}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}]-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+4+32-\\frac{121}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09aa9602-f624-59b4-90e5-e52ad4429899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09aa9602-f624-59b4-90e5-e52ad4429899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527325} :answer-field/id #uuid "557ab1f0-6633-5399-9cab-8d702abcc76a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ac560de-8de4-5482-a34a-4c1b0b94d0a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-266630" :question/problem "$6\\operatorname{sec}⁡{60}^{∘}+3\\operatorname{csc}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡{30}^{∘}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $sec$  is the reciprocal of $cos,$  $csc$ is the reciprocal of $sin$  and $cot$  is the reciprocal of $tan.$\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}},\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}}.\n$$\n\nThe angle ${60}^{∘}$ and ${30}^{∘}$ are special, and its trigonometric ratios have the values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{sec}⁡{60}^{∘}+3\\operatorname{csc}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡{30}^{∘} &= 6(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}})+3(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}})+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}} \\\\ &= \\frac{6}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}}+\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}} \\\\ &= \\frac{6}{(\\frac{1}{2})}+\\frac{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}+\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})} \\\\ &= 12+6+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ &= 18+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ &= 18+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ &= 18+\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527932 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e20a1e7-c143-53bc-878e-ea30164f1481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527337} {:answer/id #uuid "634b9a3d-06ab-59c4-8af6-665347876113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527495} {:answer/id #uuid "68106bf3-93d0-57f1-baec-9d90f724b12b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527505} {:answer/id #uuid "95fc6287-af80-5279-939b-bc5232fd84f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527606} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e1a5fe-ed76-5dc0-83b1-3c95dde85b9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6e1a5fe-ed76-5dc0-83b1-3c95dde85b9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527707} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0780126-c4c2-5d32-bb52-f4490db11809" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41ffd91c-a5ef-5456-865a-acafdbd924a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-146245" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=3]$ given that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|3}=\\frac{1}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $X,$ corresponding to the row totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{4}{9}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x:$\n\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=3] & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)]-{\\mu }_{Y|3}^{2} \\\\  & =[{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+{1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}]-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527934 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2af6dba2-cefe-540b-8ae2-f822fecac4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2af6dba2-cefe-540b-8ae2-f822fecac4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527403} :answer-field/id #uuid "377436fb-63b0-5a7f-9bbf-0d04a4c1e2f0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527824} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8aaf9d2a-7bf4-5fae-949b-2222f508d67a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8aaf9d2a-7bf4-5fae-949b-2222f508d67a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527582} :answer-field/id #uuid "703919d5-cced-54a9-9d55-c7c534870732" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43c9679e-fdc0-5c6d-a6ac-430a80de179e" :question/math-academy-id "q-350382" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)=1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})]=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})]=-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the tangent to the curve is vertical at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d126f60-c244-55e8-99ea-c6908268f2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527335} {:answer/id #uuid "1a86b6cb-7dc9-57c4-8aaa-4c6993228125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527364} {:answer/id #uuid "326e86ac-4b4d-58bd-bac0-9dc086c029d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527413} {:answer/id #uuid "bcc743be-6c0f-592e-bc22-3ef4125c7e4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527692} {:answer/id #uuid "e5fa9767-9f89-5829-885f-cdf43d1a2b7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5fa9767-9f89-5829-885f-cdf43d1a2b7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527760} :answer-field/id #uuid "e62dccaf-73d1-5442-9e53-eebdcd332113" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46e4a876-e7eb-598f-bbec-ff44b3be1391" :question/math-academy-id "q-118775" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x){|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)) \\\\  & =0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1257fcfb-46f8-5247-807c-1316aea2b0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527346} {:answer/id #uuid "498fdd26-91d0-57b8-87a1-230ef8d64f3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527451} {:answer/id #uuid "79f1dcea-b07b-564c-8376-a75167e5d009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527536} {:answer/id #uuid "a61ab541-431f-5790-8fbe-9236a28cdc2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527649} {:answer/id #uuid "b68efde0-35a7-58f7-b80d-1b1f8172c3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1257fcfb-46f8-5247-807c-1316aea2b0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527346} :answer-field/id #uuid "01436a2c-b285-56d1-972d-3781d784d175" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "48b554a2-761e-5c5b-ad17-fc83b8df8618" :question/math-academy-id "q-146274" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{10}{27}$ | $\\frac{5}{27}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{27}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{27}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=9]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|9}=\\frac{9}{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution of $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{10}{27}$ | $\\frac{5}{27}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{27}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{27}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{7}{27}$ | $\\frac{13}{27}$ | $\\frac{7}{27}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y:$\n\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{6}{7}$ | $\\frac{10}{13}$ | $\\frac{5}{7}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{3}{13}$ | $\\frac{2}{7}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=9] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,9)]-{\\mu }_{X|9}^{2} \\\\  & =[{1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{7}+{2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{7}]-{(\\frac{9}{7})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{7}+\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{81}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c9eaf20-c666-5150-bbf7-5cdd25f3858a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-5=6(x-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527371} {:answer/id #uuid "23a36e5a-2d17-540d-a480-934ae5ff5398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-5=\\sqrt{2}(x-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527387} {:answer/id #uuid "51715b4c-e8ae-5dee-8559-fabf91340f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+5=2\\sqrt{3}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527462} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e00b9e-7f09-5e90-bc56-bbe95b5482c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-5=6\\sqrt{2}(x-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527689} {:answer/id #uuid "fdfe79b9-6624-5d5d-a154-82e31819e4aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+\\sqrt{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}(x+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8e00b9e-7f09-5e90-bc56-bbe95b5482c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-5=6\\sqrt{2}(x-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527689} :answer-field/id #uuid "856fdf82-8f4f-508d-812e-b4bde879e8dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5579ca0e-f803-5d9f-87c0-8c8636fd4e81" :question/math-academy-id "q-122584" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t+1},\\,y={t}^{3}+4,\\,-1<t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{1+1}=\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ y & =(1)^{3}+4=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(\\sqrt{2},5).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t+1}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t+1}})} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{(t+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =6\\cdot {1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(\\sqrt{2},5)$ and the slope $m=6\\sqrt{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5 & =6\\sqrt{2}(x-\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
